Edition No. 31 — Sunday, 1 November 2026

10 stories: Petrol K33.06, diesel K34.45: the ERB raises every fuel price for November on a weaker kwacha and Gulf tensions, a day after Nalumango ruled out using reserves at the pump (lead); No ballot in Mandevu: the ECZ will declare Adrian Banda MP on 5 November, Tonse asks for a rerun with a new candidate, and Nalumango says a retreating opposition could gift 2031; Fertiliser, irrigation and 10,000 megawatts: in Moscow, Haimbe reads Hichilema's pitch to Russian investors and asks for help with climate preparedness and African-language AI; 12,637 cases in ninety days: police record a rise in gender-based violence for the third quarter, with 2,751 child victims, 49 murders and Lusaka and Luapula top of the table; 'Not a registered entity': the Chitimukulu asks the Constitutional Court to throw out the petition to make Chief Mukuni a paramount chief, saying the committee behind it cannot sue; Africa's first oral cholera vaccine plant breaks ground in November, the President says, as the outbreak that prompted a 709,000-dose campaign stands at 1,622 cases and 30 deaths; Champions of Tier B: Zambia's under-15s beat Portugal 5–2 in Baku, Maxen Msimuko scores a hat-trick in the final, and Keith Mweemba tells the squad FAZ will manage what comes next; Two from two at NASDEC: Munali Suns beat Bravehearts and come from ten down against Beira, and meet Botswana's Dolphins on Sunday afternoon with a place in the Elite 16 the prize; A K100,000 fine and a ban held in reserve: FAZ's disciplinary committee punishes Nkana for the political chants of 10 October but suspends the five-match exile from Kitwe; A debate at Kaunda's old house earns two Lusaka schools a trip to Uganda, and the heritage commission says it turned a complaint about motorbikes into an exchange programme.


